WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.680
Let u be any positive integer.

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:05.380
We combine the exact sign
formula with Gaussian parity.

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:11.010
E of P equals minus H three of P, plus
the product of the quantity i minus one

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:15.400
with the half-sum of H one and H two.
The half-sum has integer coefficients.

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:21.200
Since i minus one equals i multiplied
by the quantity one plus i,

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:24.760
the whole second term is
divisible by one plus i.

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:28.760
Under coefficient reduction
it vanishes, leaving E of P

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:32.460
congruent to minus H three
of P. The exact integer

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:36.800
half-sum was formed before this reduction;
we are not dividing in the residue ring.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:42.740
Write H three as the sum of h
sub three r times P to the r,

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:47.920
for r from zero to u squared minus
one. Each sign is plus or minus one.

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:51.490
Both possible values of its
negative have residue one.

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:54.500
Thus E of P is congruent to J of P,

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:57.340
the sum of P to the r
over those same positions.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:02.360
Every coefficient of E minus
J is divisible by one plus i.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:07.300
This uses the common signs, without
norm identities or oddness of u.

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:11.840
For u equal to three, there
are nine coefficient positions.

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:16.933
Here is an illustrative row of
allowed signs for minus H three.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:20.920
Keep all nine powers, from P to the
zero through P to the eighth, fixed.

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:24.780
Reduce each coefficient to
one. These are residues:

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:28.850
the original coefficients
of E need not equal one.

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:34.170
Now substitute P equal to one.
Every power in J becomes one.

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:39.520
Therefore E of one is congruent to J
of one, and J of one equals u squared.

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:44.360
This evaluation statement is different
from the polynomial congruence we

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:48.700
have already proved. Only
now assume that u is odd.

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:52.220
Then u squared is odd.
In the nine-term example,

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:57.100
four pairs of ones vanish because two
has residue zero, and one remains.

00:01:57.740 --> 00:02:00.980
In general write u
squared as two k plus one.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:04.690
The k pairs vanish in
characteristic two, leaving one.

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:09.180
Hence E of one is congruent
to one modulo one plus i.

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:12.900
Carry forward the coefficient congruence,

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:16.820
together with the exact sign
divisibilities from the earlier node:

00:02:17.210 --> 00:02:20.300
two divides d of P minus c of P,

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:26.000
and one plus i divides g of P minus
c of P, coefficient by coefficient.

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:29.320
Those differences supply
the later sign comparisons.

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:33.580
The all-ones congruence will
enter the final contradiction

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:36.680
after the product identities
become an additive relation.
